# ── Configuration and security checks ───────────────────────────────────────
# Settings modules control auth backends, secret keys, and CORS policy. The
# OAuth validator decides which clients may still authenticate; the Sentry
# module decides what leaves the process.
#
# Patterns, not exact filenames, and deliberately the same shape the gate in
# .github/scripts/security-review.cjs uses: an entry that covers less than the
# gate does asks for no reviewer on a change the gate will still hold, which
# reads as the gate being broken rather than as the owner list being stale.
# `*` here does not cross a directory separator, so the recursive form is the
# one that matches the gate's `promop/**/*settings*`. The plain form stays
# beside it: both name the same owner, so the pair costs nothing, and it does
# not depend on `/**/` being read as "zero or more" directories rather than one
# or more — a reading that, if wrong, would drop the owner from settings.py
# itself.
/promop/*settings*                  @larsburgess
/promop/**/*settings*               @larsburgess
/promop/settings/                   @larsburgess
/ctomop/*settings*                  @larsburgess
/ctomop/**/*settings*               @larsburgess
/ctomop/settings/                   @larsburgess
/promop/oauth.py                    @larsburgess
/promop/oauth/                      @larsburgess
/ctomop/oauth.py                    @larsburgess
/ctomop/oauth/                      @larsburgess
/promop/sentry.py                   @larsburgess
/promop/sentry/                     @larsburgess
/ctomop/sentry.py                   @larsburgess
/ctomop/sentry/                     @larsburgess
/patient_portal/checks.py           @larsburgess
/patient_portal/checks/             @larsburgess
# Both manifests: the scanners answer "is anything here known-bad", review
# answers "should this be here at all".
/frontend/package*.json             @larsburgess
/requirements*.txt                  @larsburgess
/requirements/                      @larsburgess

